From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: GloriousEggroll <gloriouseggroll@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 00:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] winepipewire.drv: Send Bluetooth DualSense and DualShock 4
 audio through hidraw.

Port GE's winepulse.drv Bluetooth controller audio ("send DualSense
haptics and speaker audio to Bluetooth controllers" and "send DualShock 4
audio to and from Bluetooth controllers") to the PipeWire driver.

A Bluetooth Sony controller has no USB audio function, so there is no
node for its speaker, haptics or headset microphone. When winebus
presents it as USB, expose the same synthetic endpoints as winepulse,
with the same device strings and the container ID winebus gives the
controller, and bridge them through hidraw:

- DualSense: a 48 kHz four-channel endpoint; haptics resampled to 3 kHz
  and speaker or headphone audio encoded as Opus, in reports 0x32/0x36.
  Packet layout and resampling follow DS5Dongle and vDS (MIT).
- DualShock 4: a stereo 32 kHz speaker, SBC encoded in report 0x17, and
  the mono 16 kHz headset microphone. Layouts follow DS4Dongle (MPL-2.0).

The codec, mixer and report code moves to bluetooth.c unchanged apart
from the stream fields. These streams have no PipeWire stream: like in
winepulse they keep a local ring that mmdevapi's timer_loop thread drains
in real time into the per-controller mixer (or fills from the decoded
microphone), signalling the client each period. The unix calls dispatch
to them by a tag bit in the stream handle.

The hotplug watcher also re-probes when the set of hidraw devices
changes, since a Bluetooth controller adds no PipeWire node; winepulse
only picked these endpoints up when some other device changed.

PROTON_DUALSENSE_BT_HAPTICS=0, PROTON_DUALSHOCK4_BT_AUDIO=0 and the
*_BT_AS_USB and Steam Input conditions apply as in winepulse.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
